# Rust build artifacts
/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Editor / OS noise
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# Local environment
.env
.env.local

# Aristo runtime artifacts produced by dogfooding the SDK on its own source.
# `aristo.toml` and most of `.aristo/` are tracked — they carry durable project
# state (config, verify specs, the `.proof` verdict trees, generated docs) that
# must propagate to CI and fresh clones. The EXCEPTION is `.aristo/index.toml`:
# under index-as-local-cache (Option B) it is a regenerable cache, derived from
# source + `.aristo/proofs/` on demand by every read command, so it is
# gitignored. Freshness is enforced in CI by `aristo verify --audit`, not by a
# committed index. See docs/decisions/index-as-local-cache.md.
/.aristo/index.toml
#
# `.github/workflows/aristo.yml` was historically gitignored when it was a
# placeholder; slice 34 promotes it to the real gate sequence (stamp →
# lint → verify → doc → status → badge artifact) and tracks it here so CI
# actually runs the gates. Other projects generated by `aristo init` get
# a `cargo install aristo-cli`-based variant and commit theirs the same way.

# Slice 27.5 D5: review-session state is per-user runtime, not project.
# The *effects* of approvals (.proof, source/index changes)
# reach git via normal commits — but the session lifecycle file, the
# active pointer, the rejection log, and the per-kind backlog are all
# personal audit trails, gitignored so cross-team contamination is
# structurally impossible.
.aristo/sessions/

# Nudge/progress engine state (Phase 18 #9): the reviewed map, edit-window
# baseline, edit counter, and per-signal throttle. Per-user runtime — like
# `.aristo/sessions/`, the *effects* of reviews reach git via normal commits;
# this file is a personal audit trail. `aristo init` wires this ignore for
# consumer repos (see init.rs `ARISTO_GITIGNORE_ENTRIES`).
.aristo/nudge-state.toml

# Per-pipeline queue state — pending tasks, claimed-in-flight entries,
# and the workers' atomic-rename machinery. Runtime only; a fresh clone
# computes its own queue on the next `aristo verify` / `aristo critique`.
.aristo/verify-queue/
.aristo/critique-queue/

# Critique findings: `.critique` files are advisory prose suggestions,
# regenerated by each `aristo critique` run; their review dispositions live
# in the gitignored session/rejection state. Unlike `.proof` (durable
# verification results that gate trust), they are reproducible and
# ephemeral — gitignored so a critique pass doesn't add churn for transient
# advisory data. `aristo init` wires this ignore for consumer repos.
.aristo/critiques/

# SDK-internal write-protection: `aristo verify --apply-verdicts`
# writes <id>.proof.bak before overwriting <id>.proof, then the sweep
# block cleans it up on success. Orphans can survive if stamp cascades
# the .proof deletion (text-drift); they're transient by design.
.aristo/proofs/*.proof.bak

# Archived orphan proofs (Task #13 / ID-D5): when an annotation is removed
# or its id changes (reword/rename), `aristo stamp` MOVES the now-orphaned
# `.proof` here instead of deleting it, so a verdict is never lost to a
# stray stamp. It is a local recovery net — recoverable on the machine that
# stamped, purged only by the explicit `aristo stamp --gc` — so it is not
# committed. `aristo init` wires this ignore for consumer repos; the full
# runtime set lives in init.rs `ARISTO_GITIGNORE_ENTRIES`.
.aristo/archive/

# trycmd runs scenario commands in-place against `tests/cmd/active/*.in/`
# (no temp-dir isolation). Commands that write outputs (e.g.
# `aristo badge --out=docs/badge.svg`) leave artifacts behind. These
# are runtime, not source — ignore so they don't show up in git status
# after every `cargo test` run.
crates/aristo-cli/tests/cmd/active/*.in/docs/
crates/aristo-cli/tests/cmd/active/*.in/.aristo/verify-queue/
